  "step_3": "The decision reason requires (1) computing an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes, (2) identifying which outcomes produce positive differences from this level, and (3) preferring the lottery with the higher chance of such positive differences. The individual does not reference any such computation. The aspiration level (-3000) is not mentioned or implied. There is no indication that the individual compared outcomes to a reference point derived from averaging all possible outcomes. The mention of 'less loss' in Lottery B contradicts the actual loss magnitudes and seems based on frequency of zero rather than deviation from an aspiration level. While Lottery B does have a higher probability of the 0 outcome (which is the only one above the aspiration level), the individual does not justify their thought in terms of 'positive differences' or any systematic comparison to a calculated benchmark. The reasoning appears more loss-averse or frequency-based rather than aspiration-based. Therefore, there is no evidence that the individual applied the aspiration-level rule as defined.",
